<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>tree_type</text></gloss>
<definition>
<form lang="en"><text>mango tree</text></form>
<form lang="kit"><text>Gullem ddäddägatt me mängalae ttoe de kokllo ma dan gullem ddäddägatt nyäny e. (After a snake bite, the bark is quickly scratched to apply on the snake bite.)</text></form>
</definition>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Verbal Dictionary"/>
<example>
<form lang="kit"><text>manggo däg</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>bunch of mango</text></form>
</translation>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Verbal Dictionary"/>
</example>
<example>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Verbal Dictionary"/>
</example>
<example>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Verbal Dictionary"/>
</example>
<trait name ="semantic-domain-ddp4" value="1.5.1 Tree"/>
<trait name ="semantic-domain-ddp4" value="2.5.7.5 Traditional medicine"/>
<trait name ="semantic-domain-ddp4" value="5.2.3.1.2 Food from fruit"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>Also used to treat snake bite. Scrape bark of tree, rub the bitter part. The fruit was brought to South Fly from Port Moresby area, and is sometimes called Moresby Mango. There are multiple varieties of mango. Some are thick (pukepuke) others are thin (petapeta) and all are sweet (mokwang). They peak in havest in December and January.</text></form>
</note>
<note type="source">
<form lang="kit"><text>Warama Kurupel</text></form>
</note>
<illustration href="">
</illustration><relation type="Specific" ref="0d8b73fc-8f7e-4776-b5d9-fc9c7e561492"/>
